 improvements in this release.
 
 Major New Features:
+- OmniTerm keeps a code graph of every project:
+  modules, classes and functions with their
+  imports, calls and inheritance, kept current
+  in the background. Agents ask it through the
+  code_graph tool, and you can ask it with
+  `omni graph explain NAME`, `affected`, `path`
+  or `report`. Only names, paths and line numbers
+  are stored, on this Mac.
+- OmniTerm has a Projects page in its sidebar
+  listing every Forge project on your Mac, found
+  by Forge. A project’s page shows its branches,
+  worktrees, remotes, recent sessions, knowledge
+  folder, README and files. Sessions are now
+  grouped by project by default, and every
+  worktree or @head checkout counts as its
+  project; turn it off with Group by Project in
+  the sessions search menu.
+- The sidebar has a Projects mode (Shift-
+  Command-3) with your own folders of projects:
+  drag and drop, collapse, rename in place. A
+  project’s page has tabs: Home, Worktrees &
+  Branches, Sessions & Agents, Knowledge, Files
+  and Settings.
+- OmniTerm has a Settings page in its sidebar for
+  notifications, the global shortcut, login
+  startup, window behavior and defaults for new
+  agents. You can mute OmniTerm, which also
+  silences the terminal’s own notifications, and
+  choose per level, kind and agent which
+  notifications show a banner, play a sound or
+  raise the menu-bar count.
+- OmniTerm development builds add a unified local
+  Claude/Codex session catalog, transcript and
+  output search, provider-channel messages with
+  delivery receipts, and embedded semantic search.
+  Session discovery proceeds independently of
+  transcript parsing; partial content is marked.
+  Codex paginated database history includes
+  file diffs and collaboration tool results.
+  Background history and vector scans resume
+  their progress after worker restarts.
+  Native catch-up batches small histories
+  within shared byte and item limits.
+  File discovery and ingestion resume after
+  worker restarts without restarting the queue.
+  Native streams retain publication and cleanup
+  capacity while JSONL histories are catching up.
+  Status counts avoid scanning transcript bodies.
+  Requested histories gain bounded indexing
+  priority while other history keeps advancing.
+  Web-search and hook text are searchable;
+  image prompts and paths replace encoded
+  image bytes in the text index.
+  Optional scheduled memory consolidation retains
+  source-linked summaries. Scheduled inference
+  verification is still in progress.
+- Loop Agents reuse one running session and wait
+  for it to be idle before the next turn. Each
+  turn shows in history as a run of its own.
+  Optional compaction preserves a shorter working
+  context between occurrences.
 - Support for the Program Status Protocol
   (OSC 7501), which lets a program report that it
   is working, waiting on you, done, or failed.
